 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np3-01",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Hyperbole",
   "quote": "o luce magis dilecta sorori",
   "trans": "dearer to your sister than the light itself",
   "line": "31",
   "body": "Anna opens with lavish affection, loving Dido more than life. It disarms Dido before the argument begins.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-02",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "solane … carpere … noris",
   "trans": "will you waste away alone … and not know",
   "line": "32–33",
   "body": "Anna asks questions, not commands, so that Dido seems to persuade herself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-03",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Echo",
   "quote": "carpere",
   "trans": "you will be worn away",
   "line": "32",
   "body": "Anna uses the same verb as the narrator in line 2 (carpitur): Dido is being eaten away either way, by love or by loneliness.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "perpetua … iuventa",
   "trans": "throughout your youth",
   "line": "32",
   "body": "Anna stresses that Dido is still young, so a life of mourning seems a waste.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-05",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "nec dulces natos Veneris nec praemia",
   "trans": "neither sweet children nor the rewards of Venus",
   "line": "33",
   "body": "Anna offers children and love, the two things a widow gives up. The appeal is to Dido's heart, not her duty.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-06",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Dismissive tone",
   "quote": "id cinerem aut manes credis curare sepultos",
   "trans": "do you think ashes or buried spirits care about that?",
   "line": "34",
   "body": "Anna brushes aside the dead Sychaeus as mere dust. The tone is blunt and practical, the opposite of Dido's devotion.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-07",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Concession",
   "quote": "esto",
   "trans": "granted",
   "line": "35",
   "body": "\"Let it be so\": Anna concedes Dido's past refusals only to turn them against her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-08",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "non Libyae, non ante Tyro",
   "trans": "not in Libya, not before that in Tyre",
   "line": "36",
   "body": "The repeated non sweeps through her past rejections quickly, so Anna can move on to her main point.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-09",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "despectus Iarbas",
   "trans": "Iarbas, scorned",
   "line": "36",
   "body": "One word sums up Iarbas' humiliation. The rejected suitor will return later in the book as a dangerous enemy.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-10",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "placitone etiam pugnabis amori",
   "trans": "will you fight even against a love that pleases you?",
   "line": "38",
   "body": "The key question: resisting unwanted suitors was easy, but this love is welcome. pugnabis makes it sound like a pointless war against herself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-11",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "hinc … hinc",
   "trans": "on this side … on that side",
   "line": "40–42",
   "body": "Enemies are listed on both sides, so Carthage feels surrounded and in need of a protector.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "genus insuperabile bello",
   "trans": "a race unconquerable in war",
   "line": "40",
   "body": "Anna exaggerates the danger to make Aeneas seem essential to Carthage's survival.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-13",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Metaphor",
   "quote": "Numidae infreni",
   "trans": "the unbridled Numidians",
   "line": "41",
   "body": "Literally \"without bridles\": the Numidians famously rode without bridles, and the word also makes them sound wild and lawless.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-14",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "deserta siti regio",
   "trans": "a region deserted through thirst",
   "line": "42",
   "body": "Even the land is hostile, a desert empty for lack of water.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-15",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Praeteritio",
   "quote": "quid bella Tyro surgentia dicam germanique minas",
   "trans": "why should I speak of wars rising from Tyre, and our brother's threats?",
   "line": "43–44",
   "body": "Anna says she will not mention the threat from Pygmalion, and so mentions it. It is a clever way to end on the most personal danger.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-16",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Iarbas",
   "quote": "Iarbas",
   "trans": "Iarbas",
   "line": "36",
   "body": "A North African king, said to be a son of Jupiter Ammon, who sold Dido the land for Carthage and wanted to marry her. Her refusal makes him an enemy.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Gaetulians and Numidians",
   "quote": "Gaetulae … Numidae",
   "trans": "Gaetulian … Numidians",
   "line": "40–41",
   "body": "Peoples of the North African interior. The Numidians were famous horsemen, later allies and then enemies of Rome.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np3-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Syrtis and the Barcaeans",
   "quote": "Syrtis … Barcaei",
   "trans": "the Syrtis … the Barcaeans",
   "line": "41–43",
   "body": "The Syrtes were shallow, shifting sandbanks off the Libyan coast, feared by sailors. Barce was a city in Cyrenaica, to the east.",
   "exam": ""
