 "questions": [
  "How does Anna's speech differ from Dido's in tone and argument? Which of her arguments are emotional and which are practical?",
  "Is Anna a good sister in this scene? Consider what she knows and what the reader knows.",
  "How does Virgil use the list of Carthage's enemies in lines 40–44 to make an argument for the marriage?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Anna as adviser",
   "body": "<p>Anna plays the part that a nurse or confidante plays in Greek tragedy: the loving helper whose advice, given in good faith, leads the heroine to ruin. Her speech is a skilful piece of persuasion, mixing emotional appeals (youth, children, love) with hard political ones (enemies on every side).</p><p>The peoples she names were real neighbours of Carthage in Virgil's day: the Gaetulians and Numidians of the interior, the dangerous sandbanks of the Syrtes off the coast, and the people of Barce in Cyrenaica. Rome had fought long wars in North Africa, and Virgil's readers would know these names.</p>"
  }
 ]
